{
  "requirement": "The SDK offers this `InspectionContext` method and exposes it to the agent\nas the `read_node` command.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<'d, D: Domain> InspectionContext<'d, D> {\n    /// Read the contents of this graph node.\n    pub fn read(&self, node: &D::NodeId) -> Result<D::NodeContent, D::Error>;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Structural obligations.** The requirement has two. The first is a public method `InspectionContext<'d, D: Domain>::read(&self, node: &D::NodeId) -> Result<D::NodeContent, D::Error>`. The second is that the SDK exposes it to the agent as the `read_node` command.\n\n**Method signature.** `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs#impl[InspectionContext<'d, D>]/fn[read]` matches the required signature exactly.\n- It sits in `impl<'d, D: Domain> InspectionContext<'d, D>`.\n- It is `pub`, takes `&self` and `&D::NodeId`, and returns `Result<D::NodeContent, D::Error>`.\n- Its body returns `self.read_snapshot(node)?.content`.\n- `read_snapshot` returns `Result<Snapshot<D::NodeContent>, D::Error>`, so the types line up.\n\n**Agent exposure.** `crates/habeas-sdk/src/agent/tools.rs#impl[GraphSession<'_, '_, D>/Conversation]/fn[tools]` declares a tool definition named `read_node`. It takes a `path` object holding `segments`. `crates/habeas-sdk/src/agent/tools.rs#impl[GraphSession<'_, '_, D>/Conversation]/fn[dispatch]` handles the `\"read_node\"` command. It does this by calling `self.inspection.prepare_read` on the same `InspectionContext`.\n\n**Observation.** The dispatcher does not call `read` itself. It calls `prepare_read`, the helper that `read_snapshot` and `read` are built on. It records the observation when a page is delivered, which the separate `read_node.behavior` requirement covers. I read the \"exposes it … as the `read_node` command\" wording as satisfied by this. The command is backed by the same context operation under the name `read_node`. The requirement does not demand that the dispatcher call `read` itself.\n\n**Gaps.** I found no missing or conflicting structural element, so I report no gap. I found no `{fixme}` acknowledgments. The behavior and inputs of the command belong to the separate `read_node.behavior` and `read_node.inputs` requirements, not to this one.",
